.TH BOSWAS-TERMINAL 1 "November 2026" "Boswas OS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
boswas\-terminal \- the terminal emulator of Boswas OS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B boswas\-terminal
.RB [ \-\-working\-directory
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-title
.IR TITLE ]
.RB [ \-\-profile
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-new\-window ]
.RB [ \-\-separate ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR COMMAND " [" ARGUMENT ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B boswas\-terminal
shows the command line in a window with tabs.
Each tab runs one program on a pseudo\-terminal: your login shell, the
command of a profile, or the command given after
.BR \-e .
The terminal understands the control sequences of an xterm with 256 and
24\-bit colours
.RB ( TERM=xterm\-256color ,
.BR COLORTERM=truecolor ),
so editors, pagers, terminal multiplexers and remote shells work as usual,
including the alternate screen, the mouse, bracketed paste and window
titles.
.PP
When the terminal is already running, starting
.B boswas\-terminal
again opens a new tab in its active window (or a new window with
.BR \-\-new\-window )
and returns at once; it does not wait for the command to end.
.PP
A tab shows the title its program sets. Lines that scroll off the top are
kept in the scrollback, which can be searched. Addresses in the text open
with Ctrl+click. Text is selected by dragging: a double\-click selects a
word, a triple\-click a line, dragging with Alt a rectangle; Shift with the
mouse selects even while a program uses the mouse itself.
.PP
The colour scheme "Boswas Prism" follows the dark or light scheme of the
desktop. Profiles (program, starting directory, font, colour scheme,
cursor, scrollback, bell, what happens when the program ends) and the
other settings are edited in the Settings tab; changes apply at once.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-working\-directory " DIR"
Start the program in
.IR DIR .
Without this option a tab starts in the starting directory of its profile,
else where
.B boswas\-terminal
was started.
.TP
.BI \-\-title " TITLE"
Give the tab a fixed title; the program in it cannot change it.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " NAME"
Use the profile called
.I NAME
instead of the default profile.
.TP
.B \-\-new\-window
Open a new window instead of a tab.
.TP
.B \-\-separate
Run as a process of its own instead of using the running terminal.
.TP
.BI \-e " COMMAND \fR[\fIARGUMENT\fR...]"
Run
.I COMMAND
instead of the shell. Everything after
.B \-e
belongs to the command, so this option comes last. The command is started
directly, not through a shell; a single argument that contains spaces is
split into words the way a shell would quote them.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-\-version
Show the options or the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+T
New tab, in the directory of the current tab.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+N
New window.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+W
Close the tab.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+D
Duplicate the tab.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+1 ... 9
New tab with the first ... ninth profile.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Tab, Ctrl+Shift+Tab
Next tab, previous tab.
.TP
.B Alt+1 ... 8, Alt+9
A tab by position, the last tab.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+C, Ctrl+Shift+V
Copy, paste.
.TP
.B Shift+Insert
Paste.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+A
Select all.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+F
Find in the scrollback.
.TP
.B Shift+PageUp, Shift+PageDown
Scroll a page.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+Up, Ctrl+Shift+Down
Scroll a line.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+Home, Ctrl+Shift+End
Scroll to the top, to the bottom.
.TP
.B Ctrl+=, Ctrl+\-, Ctrl+0
Larger, smaller, normal text.
.TP
.B F11
Full screen.
.TP
.B Ctrl+,
Settings.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+Space
The menu of profiles.
.PP
With text selected, Ctrl+C copies it (this can be turned off); otherwise
Ctrl+C reaches the program. The middle mouse button pastes the primary
selection.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The program in a tab gets the environment of the terminal with
.BR TERM=xterm\-256color ,
.BR COLORTERM=truecolor ,
.B TERM_PROGRAM=boswas\-terminal
and
.B SHELL
set to your login shell.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/com.boswas.Terminal/settings.json
Profiles and settings.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/com.boswas.Terminal/window.ini
The size of the window.
.TP
.I /usr/share/boswas/terminal/bashrc
When this file exists and the shell is
.BR bash (1),
the shell is started with it as its start\-up file; it reads
.I ~/.bashrc
first.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the terminal ran or passed its request on to the running one, 2 for
a wrong command line, 4 when started as root.
.SH NOTES
Programs may write to the clipboard with the OSC 52 sequence only when
that is allowed in the settings; reading the clipboard this way is never
answered. Pasted text is stripped of control characters.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1),
.BR terminfo (5),
.BR xterm (1)
